#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715136 :question/problem "Given $f(x,y)=x + 2 + \\frac{8}{y - 3} - \\frac{y - 3}{x + 2}$, find the points $(x,y)$ where $\\nabla f=\\mathbf 0$. The domain is $x\\ne -2,\\ y\\ne 3$.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A gradient vanishes exactly when every component vanishes. Differentiate with respect to each variable, holding the others constant.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}&=1 + \\frac{y - 3}{\\left(x + 2\\right)^{2}} \\\\ f_{y}&=- \\frac{8}{\\left(y - 3\\right)^{2}} - \\frac{1}{x + 2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet all components equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 + \\frac{y - 3}{\\left(x + 2\\right)^{2}}&=0 \\\\ - \\frac{8}{\\left(y - 3\\right)^{2}} - \\frac{1}{x + 2}&=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo write the simultaneous equations compactly, let $u=x + 2,\\ v=y - 3$. The first equation gives $v=-u^2/1$. Because $u,v\\ne0$, substitution in the second and multiplication by $u^4$ are valid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8+u^{3}&=0 \\\\ u^3&=-8 \\\\ u&=-2 \\\\ v&=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Recover the original coordinates using $x=u - 2,\\ y=v + 3$.\n\nTherefore, the complete set of points in the domain is $(-4,-1)$. Each satisfies every component equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715143 :question/problem "Given $f(x,y)=\\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} - 2 \\left(x - 1\\right) \\cos{\\left(2 y - 6 \\right)}$, find the points $(x,y)$ where $\\nabla f=\\mathbf 0$. The domain is $3\\le y<\\frac{\\pi}{2} + 3$.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A gradient vanishes exactly when every component vanishes. Differentiate with respect to each variable, holding the others constant.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}&=2 x - 2 \\cos{\\left(2 y - 6 \\right)} - 2 \\\\ f_{y}&=4 \\left(x - 1\\right) \\sin{\\left(2 y - 6 \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet all components equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 x - 2 \\cos{\\left(2 y - 6 \\right)} - 2&=0 \\\\ 4 \\left(x - 1\\right) \\sin{\\left(2 y - 6 \\right)}&=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo write the simultaneous equations compactly, let $u=x - 1,\\ v=y - 3$. The first equation gives $u=\\cos{\\left(2 v \\right)}$. After substitution and division by the nonzero constant $4$, the second equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sin{\\left(2 v \\right)} \\cos{\\left(2 v \\right)}&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA product is zero if at least one factor is zero. In $0\\le 2 v<\\pi$, this gives $v=0$ or $v=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$. The corresponding values of $u$ are $1$ and $0$, respectively. The upper boundary is excluded. Recover the original coordinates using $x=u + 1,\\ y=v + 3$.\n\nTherefore, the complete set of points in the domain is $(2,3)$ and $(1,\\frac{\\pi}{4} + 3)$. Each satisfies every component equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715181 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x + \\sqrt{2} \\sqrt{y}$ along the line segment from $(4,0)$ to $(4,8)$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{t_a}^{t_b} f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(4,0)$ to $(4,8).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=(4)\\,i+(0)\\,j$ and $b=(4)\\,i+(8)\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(4)\\,i+(0)\\,j+t((0)\\,i+(8)\\,j) \\\\  & =(4)\\,i+(8 \\cdot t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=4$ and $y=8 \\cdot t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t)$ and $\\|r\\prime(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(4)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(8 \\cdot t)j \\\\  & =(0)\\,i+(8)\\,j \\\\ \\|r\\prime(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^2+(8)^2} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x + \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4+\\sqrt{2(8 \\cdot t)} \\\\  & =4 \\cdot \\sqrt{t} + 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite $I=\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_0^1 f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_0^1 (4 \\cdot \\sqrt{t} + 4)\\cdot 8\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =32\\int_0^1 (\\sqrt{t} + 1)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =32[\\frac{2 \\cdot t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3} + t]_0^1 \\\\  & =32(1+\\frac{2}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{160}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715185 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x y$ along the line segment from $(-1,-2)$ to $(-3,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{t_a}^{t_b} f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,-2)$ to $(-3,-1).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=(-1)\\,i+(-2)\\,j$ and $b=(-3)\\,i+(-1)\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-1)\\,i+(-2)\\,j+t((-2)\\,i+(1)\\,j) \\\\  & =(- 2 \\cdot t - 1)\\,i+(t - 2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=- 2 \\cdot t - 1$ and $y=t - 2$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t)$ and $\\|r\\prime(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- 2 \\cdot t - 1)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t - 2)j \\\\  & =(-2)\\,i+(1)\\,j \\\\ \\|r\\prime(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^2+(1)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x \\cdot y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1(- 2 \\cdot t - 1)(t - 2) \\\\  & =- 2 \\cdot t^{2} + 3 \\cdot t + 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite $I=\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_0^1 f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_0^1 (- 2 \\cdot t^{2} + 3 \\cdot t + 2)\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\int_0^1 (- 2 \\cdot t^{2} + 3 \\cdot t + 2)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[- \\frac{2 \\cdot t^{3}}{3} + \\frac{3 \\cdot t^{2}}{2} + 2 \\cdot t]_0^1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}(\\left(- \\frac{2}{3}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)+(2))-\\sqrt{5}(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{17 \\cdot \\sqrt{5}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715186 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=e^{2 x - y}$ along the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-3,-4)$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{t_a}^{t_b} f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-3,-4).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=(0)\\,i+(0)\\,j$ and $b=(-3)\\,i+(-4)\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(0)\\,i+(0)\\,j+t((-3)\\,i+(-4)\\,j) \\\\  & =(- 3 \\cdot t)\\,i+(- 4 \\cdot t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=- 3 \\cdot t$ and $y=- 4 \\cdot t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t)$ and $\\|r\\prime(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- 3 \\cdot t)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- 4 \\cdot t)j \\\\  & =(-3)\\,i+(-4)\\,j \\\\ \\|r\\prime(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^2+(-4)^2} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=e^{2 \\cdot x - y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =e^{2(- 3 \\cdot t)+-1(- 4 \\cdot t)} \\\\  & =e^{- 2 \\cdot t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe exponent has derivative $-2$. With $u=- 2 t$, $\\mathrm{d}u=-2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$; the new bounds are $0$ and $-2$. Write $I=\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_0^1 f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_0^1 (e^{- 2 \\cdot t})\\cdot 5\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =5\\int_0^1 (e^{- 2 \\cdot t})\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =5[- \\frac{e^{- 2 \\cdot t}}{2}]_0^1 \\\\  & =5\\left(- \\frac{1}{2 \\cdot e^{2}}\\right)-5\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} - \\frac{5}{2 \\cdot e^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715208 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=e^{x - 2 y}$ along the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(2,3)$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{t_a}^{t_b} f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(2,3).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=(0)\\,i+(0)\\,j$ and $b=(2)\\,i+(3)\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(0)\\,i+(0)\\,j+t((2)\\,i+(3)\\,j) \\\\  & =(2 \\cdot t)\\,i+(3 \\cdot t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2 \\cdot t$ and $y=3 \\cdot t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t)$ and $\\|r\\prime(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 \\cdot t)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 \\cdot t)j \\\\  & =(2)\\,i+(3)\\,j \\\\ \\|r\\prime(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2)^2+(3)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=e^{x - 2 \\cdot y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =e^{1(2 \\cdot t)+-2(3 \\cdot t)} \\\\  & =e^{- 4 \\cdot t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe exponent has derivative $-4$. With $u=- 4 t$, $\\mathrm{d}u=-4\\,\\mathrm{d}t$; the new bounds are $0$ and $-4$. Write $I=\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_0^1 f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_0^1 (e^{- 4 \\cdot t})\\cdot \\sqrt{13}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\int_0^1 (e^{- 4 \\cdot t})\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}[- \\frac{e^{- 4 \\cdot t}}{4}]_0^1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\left(- \\frac{1}{4 \\cdot e^{4}}\\right)-\\sqrt{13}\\left(- \\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{4 \\cdot e^{4}}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715211 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x + \\sqrt{y}$ along the line segment from $(3,0)$ to $(3,4)$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{t_a}^{t_b} f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(3,0)$ to $(3,4).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=(3)\\,i+(0)\\,j$ and $b=(3)\\,i+(4)\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(3)\\,i+(0)\\,j+t((0)\\,i+(4)\\,j) \\\\  & =(3)\\,i+(4 \\cdot t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=3$ and $y=4 \\cdot t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t)$ and $\\|r\\prime(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(4 \\cdot t)j \\\\  & =(0)\\,i+(4)\\,j \\\\ \\|r\\prime(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^2+(4)^2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x + \\sqrt{y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3+\\sqrt{1(4 \\cdot t)} \\\\  & =2 \\cdot \\sqrt{t} + 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite $I=\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_0^1 f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_0^1 (2 \\cdot \\sqrt{t} + 3)\\cdot 4\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =12\\int_0^1 (\\frac{2 \\cdot \\sqrt{t}}{3} + 1)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =12[\\frac{4 \\cdot t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{9} + t]_0^1 \\\\  & =12(1+\\frac{4}{9})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715231 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=- x - 2 y$ along the line segment from $(3,-3)$ to $(3,-5)$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{t_a}^{t_b} f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(3,-3)$ to $(3,-5).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=(3)\\,i+(-3)\\,j$ and $b=(3)\\,i+(-5)\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(3)\\,i+(-3)\\,j+t((0)\\,i+(-2)\\,j) \\\\  & =(3)\\,i+(- 2 \\cdot t - 3)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=3$ and $y=- 2 \\cdot t - 3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t)$ and $\\|r\\prime(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- 2 \\cdot t - 3)j \\\\  & =(0)\\,i+(-2)\\,j \\\\ \\|r\\prime(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^2+(-2)^2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=- x - 2 \\cdot y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =-1(3)+-2(- 2 \\cdot t - 3) \\\\  & =4 \\cdot t + 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite $I=\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_0^1 f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_0^1 (4 \\cdot t + 3)\\cdot 2\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =2\\int_0^1 (4 \\cdot t + 3)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =2[2 \\cdot t^{2} + 3 \\cdot t]_0^1 \\\\  & =2((2)+(3))-2(0) \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715232 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=- x - 2 y$ along the line segment from $(0,2)$ to $(0,4)$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{t_a}^{t_b} f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,2)$ to $(0,4).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=(0)\\,i+(2)\\,j$ and $b=(0)\\,i+(4)\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(0)\\,i+(2)\\,j+t((0)\\,i+(2)\\,j) \\\\  & =(0)\\,i+(2 \\cdot t + 2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=0$ and $y=2 \\cdot t + 2$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t)$ and $\\|r\\prime(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(0)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 \\cdot t + 2)j \\\\  & =(0)\\,i+(2)\\,j \\\\ \\|r\\prime(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^2+(2)^2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=- x - 2 \\cdot y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =-1(0)+-2(2 \\cdot t + 2) \\\\  & =- 4 \\cdot t - 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite $I=\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_0^1 f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_0^1 (- 4 \\cdot t - 4)\\cdot 2\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =2\\int_0^1 (- 4 \\cdot t - 4)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =2[- 2 \\cdot t^{2} - 4 \\cdot t]_0^1 \\\\  & =2((-2)+(-4))-2(0) \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715237 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x - y$ along the line segment from $(0,3)$ to $(3,3)$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{t_a}^{t_b} f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,3)$ to $(3,3).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=(0)\\,i+(3)\\,j$ and $b=(3)\\,i+(3)\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(0)\\,i+(3)\\,j+t((3)\\,i+(0)\\,j) \\\\  & =(3 \\cdot t)\\,i+(3)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=3 \\cdot t$ and $y=3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t)$ and $\\|r\\prime(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 \\cdot t)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3)j \\\\  & =(3)\\,i+(0)\\,j \\\\ \\|r\\prime(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3)^2+(0)^2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x - y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1(3 \\cdot t)+-1(3) \\\\  & =3 \\cdot t - 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite $I=\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_0^1 f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_0^1 (3 \\cdot t - 3)\\cdot 3\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =3\\int_0^1 (3 \\cdot t - 3)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =3[\\frac{3 \\cdot t^{2}}{2} - 3 \\cdot t]_0^1 \\\\  & =3(\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)+(-3))-3(0) \\\\  & =- \\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715244 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x + y$ along the line segment from $(0,-3)$ to $(0,-5)$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{t_a}^{t_b} f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,-3)$ to $(0,-5).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=(0)\\,i+(-3)\\,j$ and $b=(0)\\,i+(-5)\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(0)\\,i+(-3)\\,j+t((0)\\,i+(-2)\\,j) \\\\  & =(0)\\,i+(- 2 \\cdot t - 3)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=0$ and $y=- 2 \\cdot t - 3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t)$ and $\\|r\\prime(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(0)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- 2 \\cdot t - 3)j \\\\  & =(0)\\,i+(-2)\\,j \\\\ \\|r\\prime(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^2+(-2)^2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x + y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1(0)+1(- 2 \\cdot t - 3) \\\\  & =- 2 \\cdot t - 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite $I=\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_0^1 f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_0^1 (- 2 \\cdot t - 3)\\cdot 2\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =2\\int_0^1 (- 2 \\cdot t - 3)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =2[- t^{2} - 3 \\cdot t]_0^1 \\\\  & =2((-1)+(-3))-2(0) \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715247 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\left(2 x + 2\\right) e^{x y}$ along the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(2,4)$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{t_a}^{t_b} f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(2,4).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=(1)\\,i+(3)\\,j$ and $b=(2)\\,i+(4)\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(1)\\,i+(3)\\,j+t((1)\\,i+(1)\\,j) \\\\  & =(t + 1)\\,i+(t + 3)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=t + 1$ and $y=t + 3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t)$ and $\\|r\\prime(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t + 1)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t + 3)j \\\\  & =(1)\\,i+(1)\\,j \\\\ \\|r\\prime(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1)^2+(1)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\left(2 \\cdot x + 2\\right) \\cdot e^{x \\cdot y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2(t + 1)+2)e^{(t + 1)(t + 3)} \\\\  & =(2 \\cdot t + 4)e^{t^{2} + 4 \\cdot t + 3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite $I=\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_0^1 f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_0^1 ((2 \\cdot t + 4)e^{t^{2} + 4 \\cdot t + 3})\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\int_0^1 ((2 \\cdot t + 4)e^{t^{2} + 4 \\cdot t + 3})\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=t^{2} + 4 \\cdot t + 3,$ $\\mathrm{d}u=(2 \\cdot t + 4)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\sqrt{2}\\int_{3}^{8}e^u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}[e^u]_{3}^{8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(e^{8}-e^{3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\left(- e^{3} + e^{8}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715257 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\sec^{2}{\\left(\\frac{\\pi \\left(- x + y\\right)}{12} \\right)}$ along the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(1,-2)$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{t_a}^{t_b} f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(1,-2).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=(0)\\,i+(0)\\,j$ and $b=(1)\\,i+(-2)\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(0)\\,i+(0)\\,j+t((1)\\,i+(-2)\\,j) \\\\  & =(t)\\,i+(- 2 \\cdot t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=t$ and $y=- 2 \\cdot t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t)$ and $\\|r\\prime(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- 2 \\cdot t)j \\\\  & =(1)\\,i+(-2)\\,j \\\\ \\|r\\prime(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1)^2+(-2)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\sec^{2}{\\left(\\frac{\\pi \\cdot \\left(- x + y\\right)}{12} \\right)}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\sec^2\\left(\\frac{\\pi((- 2 \\cdot t)-(t))}{12}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sec^2\\left(- \\frac{\\pi \\cdot t}{4}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite $I=\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_0^1 f(r(t))\\,\\|r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\int_0^1 (\\sec^2\\left(- \\frac{\\pi \\cdot t}{4}\\right))\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\int_0^1 (\\sec^2\\left(- \\frac{\\pi \\cdot t}{4}\\right))\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=- \\frac{\\pi \\cdot t}{4},$ $\\mathrm{d}u=\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =- \\frac{4 \\cdot \\sqrt{5}}{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}}\\sec^2 u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =- \\frac{4 \\cdot \\sqrt{5}}{\\pi}[\\tan u]_{0}^{- \\frac{\\pi}{4}} \\\\  & =- \\frac{4 \\cdot \\sqrt{5}}{\\pi}(\\tan\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)-\\tan(0)) \\\\  & =- \\frac{4 \\cdot \\sqrt{5}}{\\pi}(-1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4 \\cdot \\sqrt{5}}{\\pi}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715378 :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -2,4\\rangle$ is shown below.\n\n![Vector from the origin to A at (-2,4), with perpendicular horizontal and vertical legs.](images/cd/cd458eeb41ee09d6a4f58bc343575d46e3d362dc3edca40a47e1a7381fcc9bd2.png)\n\nUse $|\\mathbf{a}|=\\sqrt{a_x^2+a_y^2}$ to find its exact magnitude." :question/worked-solution "The perpendicular legs have lengths $|-2|=2$ and $|4|=4$. The vector is the hypotenuse, so the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\mathbf{a}|&=\\sqrt{a_x^2+a_y^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{(-2)^2+4^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{4+16}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA negative horizontal component describes direction; it does not make the length negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715383 :question/problem "For $\\mathbf{v}=\\left\\langle-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right\\rangle$, a student writes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\mathbf{v}|&=\\sqrt{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)^2} &&\\text{(1)}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{5}{4}} &&\\text{(2)}\\\\\n&=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{5}}{2} &&\\text{(3)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich option identifies the first incorrect line and gives the correct exact magnitude?" :question/worked-solution "Line (1) correctly applies the magnitude formula. Line (2) is also correct: squaring the negative component gives $\\frac34$, and squaring the other gives $\\frac54$.\n\nLine (3) is the first error. A square root does not distribute over addition. Add the squared components before taking the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\mathbf{v}|&=\\sqrt{\\frac34+\\frac54}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{\\frac84}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe answer is **line (3), with magnitude $\\sqrt{2}$**. As a check, the student's proposed result has square\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt3+\\sqrt5}{2}\\right)^2=2+\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}2>2,\n$$\n\nso it cannot be the magnitude whose square is $\\frac34+\\frac54=2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715386 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{b}=-\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{j}$, where $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}$ are perpendicular unit basis vectors. Find the exact magnitude of $\\mathbf{b}$." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal and vertical components are $b_x=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $b_y=\\frac{1}{2}$. Apply the magnitude formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\mathbf{b}|&=\\sqrt{b_x^2+b_y^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{\\frac{10}{4}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare each entire component, including its denominator, before adding. The magnitude is the nonnegative square root."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715442 :question/problem "![](images/b1/b1647eca6749e33ba089d65bacaf5fda8b5bef863b023c8fb8eee423edac12cd.png)\n\nLet $A(-2,3)$ and $B(1,-1)$. Find $|\\overrightarrow{AB}|$. Give the exact answer; do not round.\n\n$|\\overrightarrow{AB}|=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The position vectors are $\\overrightarrow{OA}=-2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}$.\n\nExpress the displacement as the ending position vector minus the starting position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{AB}&=\\overrightarrow{OB}-\\overrightarrow{OA}\\\\\n&=(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j})-(-2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j})\\\\\n&=(1-(-2))\\mathbf{i}+(-1-3)\\mathbf{j}\\\\\n&=3\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the magnitude formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\overrightarrow{AB}|&=\\sqrt{3^2+(-4)^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{9+16}\\\\\n&=5.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vertical displacement is negative, but its square contributes positively to the magnitude."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715444 :question/problem "![](images/dd/ddf40feed02dd07ada79aeeeab7af6e13b3a2d6da3966bfe04b2a34ad0bc2be9.png)\n\nPoints $A$ and $B$ are shown on the coordinate grid. Find $|\\overrightarrow{AB}|$. Give the exact answer; do not round.\n\n$|\\overrightarrow{AB}|=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Read the coordinates from the grid: $A(-2,3)$ and $B(4,-1)$. Their position vectors are $\\overrightarrow{OA}=-2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\overrightarrow{OB}=4\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}$.\n\nSubtract the starting position vector from the ending position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{AB}&=\\overrightarrow{OB}-\\overrightarrow{OA}\\\\\n&=(4\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j})-(-2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j})\\\\\n&=(4-(-2))\\mathbf{i}+(-1-3)\\mathbf{j}\\\\\n&=6\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow compute the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\overrightarrow{AB}|&=\\sqrt{6^2+(-4)^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{36+16}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{52}\\\\\n&=2\\sqrt{13}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe component changes are $6$ units right and $4$ units down; the magnitude is the straight-line length between the points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715447 :question/problem "![](images/2d/2d871548e615f138dd464075ade7f70c38c4ae35d6cd436b2aac1e15c7f043be.png)\n\nLet $A(1,1)$ and $B(3,4)$. The blue arrows are the position vectors $\\overrightarrow{OA}$ and $\\overrightarrow{OB}$; the orange arrow is $\\overrightarrow{AB}$.\n\nTo find $\\overrightarrow{AB}$, subtract the starting position vector from the ending position vector:\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\overrightarrow{OB}-\\overrightarrow{OA}.\n$$\n\nFor a vector with components $\\langle u,v\\rangle$, its magnitude is $\\sqrt{u^2+v^2}$. Find $|\\overrightarrow{AB}|$." :question/worked-solution "The position vectors are $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\overrightarrow{OB}=3\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}$.\n\nSubtract them component by component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{AB}&=\\overrightarrow{OB}-\\overrightarrow{OA}\\\\\n&=(3\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j})-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})\\\\\n&=2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow compute the magnitude:\n\n$$\n|\\overrightarrow{AB}|=\\sqrt{2^2+3^2}=\\sqrt{13}.\n$$\n\nThe coordinates of $B$ describe $\\overrightarrow{OB}$; they are not the components of $\\overrightarrow{AB}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715488 :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$, with magnitude $|a|=10$. To find a unit vector in the same direction, divide the vector by its magnitude: $u=a/|a|$.\n\nWhich unit vector is parallel to $a$ and points the same direction?" :question/worked-solution "Divide both components by the given magnitude, $10$:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a}{|a|}=\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{6}{10} \\\\ \\frac{8}{10}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe scalar $1/10$ is positive, so the direction is unchanged. Its length is\n\n$$\n|u|=\\sqrt{\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)^2}=\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}+\\frac{16}{25}}=1.\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715493 :question/problem "Let $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $b$ and points the same direction." :question/worked-solution "A unit vector in the same direction is $u=b/|b|$. First calculate the magnitude:\n\n$$\n|b|=\\sqrt{2^2+(-3)^2}=\\sqrt{4+9}=\\sqrt{13}.\n$$\n\nDivide both components by this magnitude:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe positive scaling preserves direction. Check the length:\n\n$$\n|u|=\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{13}+\\frac{9}{13}}=1.\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715500 :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $2a+3b$ and points the same direction." :question/worked-solution "First form the vector that must be normalized:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a+3b&=2\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind its magnitude:\n\n$$\n|2a+3b|=\\sqrt{(-1)^2+(-4)^2}=\\sqrt{1+16}=\\sqrt{17}.\n$$\n\nNow divide the entire resultant by its magnitude:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2a+3b}{|2a+3b|}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe factor $1/\\sqrt{17}$ is positive, so $u$ points the same direction as $2a+3b$. Its length is\n\n$$\n|u|=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{17}+\\frac{16}{17}}=1.\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715532 :question/problem "![](images/ed/ed3cc5a19c37cff312d79d701ae92c971ac2d64e966d8506f02a542ecb6e1fb9.png)\n\nLet $P(2,0)$ and $Q(0,4)$. Find $|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}|$. Do not round the answer.\n\n$|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}|=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OP}=p=2 i, \\\\ & \\overset{\\to }{OQ}=q=4 j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} &= \\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\ &= q-p \\\\ &= (4 j)-(2 i) \\\\ &= - 2 i + 4 j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| &= \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(4)^{2}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{4+16} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{20} \\\\ &= 2 \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715535 :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb8518f2c9afeef55db8634aea6c2baead15d1b2cef8b94d2d5ccd645682b5cd.png)\n\nLet $A(1,0)$ and $B(4,0)$. Find $|\\overset{\\to }{AB}|$. Do not round the answer.\n\n$|\\overset{\\to }{AB}|=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=i, \\\\ & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=4 i.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} &= \\overset{\\to }{OB}-\\overset{\\to }{OA} \\\\ &= b-a \\\\ &= (4 i)-(i) \\\\ &= 3 i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| &= \\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{9+0} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{9} \\\\ &= 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715541 :question/problem "![](images/14/1472c4871edf97541b56bb3390419face9062de1fb80af05a1b053763e09152b.png)\n\nLet $A(3,2)$ and $B(1,2)$. Find $|\\overset{\\to }{AB}|$. Do not round the answer.\n\n$|\\overset{\\to }{AB}|=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=3 i + 2 j, \\\\ & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=i + 2 j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} &= \\overset{\\to }{OB}-\\overset{\\to }{OA} \\\\ &= b-a \\\\ &= (i + 2 j)-(3 i + 2 j) \\\\ &= - 2 i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| &= \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{4+0} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{4} \\\\ &= 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715543 :question/problem "![](images/a4/a4401d3d4327b8f8584c492c3728a4b06979be426bb01f386d238aa64eb0e1ce.png)\n\nLet $P(4,2)$ and $Q(0,0)$. Find $|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}|$. Do not round the answer.\n\n$|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}|=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OP}=p=4 i + 2 j, \\\\ & \\overset{\\to }{OQ}=q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} &= \\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\ &= q-p \\\\ &= (0)-(4 i + 2 j) \\\\ &= - 4 i - 2 j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| &= \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{16+4} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{20} \\\\ &= 2 \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715545 :question/problem "![](images/6f/6fcfd665b9c9d117d4920149d6bf4f25f866681b11c87ccb82f7e0fc9016f88f.png)\n\nLet $A(2,2)$ and $C(4,3)$. Find $|\\overset{\\to }{AC}|$. Do not round the answer.\n\n$|\\overset{\\to }{AC}|=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=2 i + 2 j, \\\\ & \\overset{\\to }{OC}=c=4 i + 3 j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AC} &= \\overset{\\to }{OC}-\\overset{\\to }{OA} \\\\ &= c-a \\\\ &= (4 i + 3 j)-(2 i + 2 j) \\\\ &= 2 i + j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{AC}| &= \\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{4+1} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715550 :question/problem "![](images/25/25189e0d4468106954a9dd3f849bd1ebe25b8a07942a1ac576274e344d39c537.png)\n\nPoints $A$ and $C$ are shown in the picture above. Find $|\\overset{\\to }{AC}|$. Do not round the answer.\n\n$|\\overset{\\to }{AC}|=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the picture, we obtain $A(-2,1)$ and $C(-2,-2)$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=- 2 i + j, \\\\ & \\overset{\\to }{OC}=c=- 2 i - 2 j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AC} &= \\overset{\\to }{OC}-\\overset{\\to }{OA} \\\\ &= c-a \\\\ &= (- 2 i - 2 j)-(- 2 i + j) \\\\ &= - 3 j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{AC}| &= \\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{0+9} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{9} \\\\ &= 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715551 :question/problem "![](images/6d/6df8aad33a08ba5ddf6a103173c6df36175268c7325c1d9c50f1b658fe647527.png)\n\nPoints $A$ and $C$ are shown in the picture above. Find $|\\overset{\\to }{AC}|$. Do not round the answer.\n\n$|\\overset{\\to }{AC}|=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the picture, we obtain $A(2,0)$ and $C(3,4)$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=2 i, \\\\ & \\overset{\\to }{OC}=c=3 i + 4 j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AC} &= \\overset{\\to }{OC}-\\overset{\\to }{OA} \\\\ &= c-a \\\\ &= (3 i + 4 j)-(2 i) \\\\ &= i + 4 j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{AC}| &= \\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(4)^{2}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{1+16} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715557 :question/problem "![](images/1e/1e93c6c71dc1b1b5788f3e25e6892fc034912847a28f7db085fcceaec3376eb9.png)\n\nPoints $A$ and $C$ are shown in the picture above. Find $|\\overset{\\to }{AC}|$. Do not round the answer.\n\n$|\\overset{\\to }{AC}|=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the picture, we obtain $A(-4,-1)$ and $C(-3,1)$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=- 4 i - j, \\\\ & \\overset{\\to }{OC}=c=- 3 i + j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AC} &= \\overset{\\to }{OC}-\\overset{\\to }{OA} \\\\ &= c-a \\\\ &= (- 3 i + j)-(- 4 i - j) \\\\ &= i + 2 j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{AC}| &= \\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{1+4} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715559 :question/problem "![](images/c7/c7c2cb2ded1c3ed785714bc37a2328cba9fa5052dc0e74063a861cb71160c580.png)\n\nPoints $A$ and $B$ are shown in the picture above. Find $|\\overset{\\to }{AB}|$. Do not round the answer.\n\n$|\\overset{\\to }{AB}|=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the picture, we obtain $A(-1,-3)$ and $B(-3,3)$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=- i - 3 j, \\\\ & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=- 3 i + 3 j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} &= \\overset{\\to }{OB}-\\overset{\\to }{OA} \\\\ &= b-a \\\\ &= (- 3 i + 3 j)-(- i - 3 j) \\\\ &= - 2 i + 6 j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| &= \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(6)^{2}} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{4+36} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{40} \\\\ &= 2 \\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715612 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\left\\langle \\sqrt{7},\\sqrt{10}\\right\\rangle$. Find its exact magnitude. Enter an exact value; an equivalent unsimplified radical is accepted." :question/worked-solution "The components are $v_x=\\sqrt{7}$ and $v_y=\\sqrt{10}$. Square each entire signed component, then add.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\mathbf{v}|&=\\sqrt{v_x^2+v_y^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\sqrt{7}\\right)^2+\\left(\\sqrt{10}\\right)^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{7+10}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{17}\\\\\n&= \\sqrt{17}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude is nonnegative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715619 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\left\\langle - \\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{7}\\right\\rangle$. Find its exact magnitude. Enter an exact value; an equivalent unsimplified radical is accepted." :question/worked-solution "The components are $a_x=- \\sqrt{5}$ and $a_y=\\sqrt{7}$. Square each entire signed component, then add.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\mathbf{a}|&=\\sqrt{a_x^2+a_y^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{\\left(- \\sqrt{5}\\right)^2+\\left(\\sqrt{7}\\right)^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{5+7}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{12}\\\\\n&=\\frac{\\sqrt{4\\cdot 3}}{1}\\\\\n&= 2 \\sqrt{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude is nonnegative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715620 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\left\\langle \\sqrt{7},2 \\sqrt{2}\\right\\rangle$. Find its exact magnitude. Enter an exact value; an equivalent unsimplified radical is accepted." :question/worked-solution "The components are $v_x=\\sqrt{7}$ and $v_y=2 \\sqrt{2}$. Square each entire signed component, then add.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\mathbf{v}|&=\\sqrt{v_x^2+v_y^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\sqrt{7}\\right)^2+\\left(2 \\sqrt{2}\\right)^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{7+8}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{15}\\\\\n&= \\sqrt{15}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude is nonnegative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715623 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{u}=\\left\\langle - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\right\\rangle$. Find its exact magnitude. Enter an exact value; an equivalent unsimplified radical is accepted." :question/worked-solution "The components are $u_x=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ and $u_y=- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}$. Square each entire signed component, then add.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\mathbf{u}|&=\\sqrt{u_x^2+u_y^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)^2+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\right)^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{\\frac{2+6}{4}}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{2}\\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude is nonnegative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715625 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\left\\langle \\sqrt{3},- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right\\rangle$. Find its exact magnitude. Enter an exact value; an equivalent unsimplified radical is accepted." :question/worked-solution "The components are $a_x=\\sqrt{3}$ and $a_y=- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}$. Square each entire signed component, then add.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\mathbf{a}|&=\\sqrt{a_x^2+a_y^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\sqrt{3}\\right)^2+\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{3+\\frac{5}{4}}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{\\frac{12+5}{4}}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{4}}\\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude is nonnegative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715636 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=-5\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}$, where $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}$ are perpendicular unit basis vectors. Find its exact magnitude. Enter an exact value; an equivalent unsimplified radical is accepted." :question/worked-solution "The components are $a_x=-5$ and $a_y=4$. Square each entire signed component, then add.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\mathbf{a}|&=\\sqrt{a_x^2+a_y^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{\\left(-5\\right)^2+\\left(4\\right)^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{25+16}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{41}\\\\\n&= \\sqrt{41}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude is nonnegative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715637 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{v}=0\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}$, where $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}$ are perpendicular unit basis vectors. Find its exact magnitude. Enter an exact value; an equivalent unsimplified radical is accepted." :question/worked-solution "The components are $v_x=0$ and $v_y=6$. Square each entire signed component, then add.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\mathbf{v}|&=\\sqrt{v_x^2+v_y^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{\\left(0\\right)^2+\\left(6\\right)^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{0+36}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{36}\\\\\n&= 6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude is nonnegative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715642 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{u}=2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}$, where $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}$ are perpendicular unit basis vectors. Find its exact magnitude. Enter an exact value; an equivalent unsimplified radical is accepted." :question/worked-solution "The components are $u_x=2$ and $u_y=3$. Square each entire signed component, then add.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\mathbf{u}|&=\\sqrt{u_x^2+u_y^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{\\left(2\\right)^2+\\left(3\\right)^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{4+9}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{13}\\\\\n&= \\sqrt{13}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude is nonnegative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186715645 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=-2\\mathbf{i}-6\\mathbf{j}$, where $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}$ are perpendicular unit basis vectors. Find its exact magnitude. Enter an exact value; an equivalent unsimplified radical is accepted." :question/worked-solution "The components are $a_x=-2$ and $a_y=-6$. Square each entire signed component, then add.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\mathbf{a}|&=\\sqrt{a_x^2+a_y^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{\\left(-2\\right)^2+\\left(-6\\right)^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{4+36}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{40}\\\\\n&=\\frac{\\sqrt{4\\cdot 10}}{1}\\\\\n&= 2 \\sqrt{10}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude is nonnegative."}]}
